2026-03-31: Connected over SSH to the user-provided native x86 Linux host `10.202.3.45` as `easemob`. Verified the machine is Ubuntu 24.04.1 on `x86_64`, with 4 vCPU, 7.8 GiB RAM, and ~80G root disk. Docker was installed but the user lacked direct daemon access, so the session used `sudo` for all remote Docker operations.
2026-03-31: Prepared the remote host for offline benchmark execution by exporting local images `tendis-amd64-current:latest` and `redislabs/memtier_benchmark:latest` to `/tmp/*.tar`, uploading them over SCP, and loading them on the remote host with `docker load`. This avoided relying on registry pulls from the remote environment.
2026-03-31: Uploaded and executed `/tmp/run_write_amd64_native_20260331.sh` on the remote native amd64 host. The script used the same newer direct-harness methodology as the recent local experiments: server `--cpus=4 --memory=8g`, client `memtier_benchmark` with `-t 4 -c 50`, direct `--network container:<server>` access to `127.0.0.1:51002`, one 30s warmup and 3 x 30s measured trials per workload. Measured workloads were `SET p1` and `SET p5`.
2026-03-31: Pulled the remote result files back to local `/tmp/write-amd64-native-20260331`. The native amd64 benchmark completed successfully on this host, but the live run showed obvious write stalls and unstable tail latency, so the host/storage/runtime path is materially affecting VM write performance. Detailed numbers were saved in `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-20260331.md` and the companion `.tsv` files.
2026-03-31: Saved the native x86 benchmark summary to `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-20260331.md`, explicitly marking it as host-specific native amd64 reference data rather than a clean amd64-vs-arm64 architecture conclusion because the host differs from the earlier arm64 benchmark environment in CPU family, storage path, and virtualization conditions.
2026-03-31: Saved repo-local copies of the native amd64 benchmark tables to `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-20260331.median.tsv` and `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-20260331.raw.tsv`, expanded the markdown summary with the key runtime config values from `build-arm/tendisplus.runtime.conf`, and prepared a focused commit containing the native amd64 benchmark note, raw results, and today's agent log.
2026-03-31: Reconnected to the same native amd64 VM for infrastructure diagnosis after the benchmark showed unexpectedly poor write throughput. Verified this is a VMware full-virtualization guest. Key findings: the system disk is exposed as `VMware Virtual disk` behind `LSI53C1030` virtual SCSI, not a paravirtual storage path; kernel boot logs reported `Cache data unavailable` and `Assuming drive cache: write through`; CPU topology is exposed as `4 sockets x 1 core` instead of a more normal `1 socket x 4 cores`. Added these findings to `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-20260331.md`.
2026-03-31: Ran two lightweight host-side write checks on the native amd64 VM to distinguish bandwidth problems from sync-write latency problems. Sequential buffered throughput looked acceptable, but small synchronous writes were disproportionately slow. This strongly supports the conclusion that the VM/storage path is especially poor for small sync-heavy database writes. Exact measurements were recorded in `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-20260331.md`.
2026-03-31: Connected to a second candidate native amd64 host `10.202.3.43` (Rocky Linux 9.7). Verified it is also a full-virtualization guest, this time under Microsoft Hyper-V, with 16 vCPU, ~23 GiB RAM, Intel i9-13900KF host CPU exposure, and container runtime implemented through `podman` rather than Docker. Disk path differs from the earlier VMware guest: kernel reports `Msft Virtual Disk` via `storvsc`, with write cache enabled and graph driver `overlay`.
2026-03-31: Performed a quick suitability check on `10.202.3.43` before transferring images or running the full benchmark. Sequential writes were fast, but the small sync-write path was catastrophically slow, clearly worse than the earlier amd64 VM. Concluded that this host is not suitable for write-heavy Tendis benchmarking because storage behavior would dominate the result.
2026-03-31: Connected to a third native amd64 host `39.106.7.212` as `root`. Verified this is an Aliyun ECS KVM guest running Ubuntu 24.04.4 on `x86_64`, with 4 vCPU, about 30 GiB RAM, Intel Xeon Platinum 8369B CPU exposure, and `podman 4.9.3` as the container engine. The root filesystem is on `/dev/vda3` (`ext4`), and there is an extra empty local NVMe instance-storage disk `/dev/nvme0n1` that was intentionally left untouched per user instruction.
2026-03-31: Checked the active mounted disk path on `39.106.7.212` before benchmarking. Sequential write throughput on the current mounted disk was acceptable, but the small sync-write path was still noticeably expensive. This looked materially better than the Hyper-V VM, but still not like a clean local-NVMe benchmark target.
2026-03-31: Exported local images `tendis-amd64-current:latest` and `redislabs/memtier_benchmark:latest`, uploaded them to `39.106.7.212`, and loaded them with `podman load` to avoid relying on remote registry access. Verified the images locally on the remote host and completed a short smoke test using `podman run --network container:<server>` against `127.0.0.1:51002`.
2026-03-31: Ran the full native amd64 write benchmark on `39.106.7.212` using podman and the same direct-harness methodology as the recent local experiments. All trials completed successfully and the host produced usable native amd64 write data under the current disk path. Detailed trial outputs were saved into the benchmark artifacts and summarized in `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-aliyun-podman-20260331.md`.
2026-03-31: Saved the Aliyun ECS native amd64 benchmark to `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-aliyun-podman-20260331.md` with repo-local result tables `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-aliyun-podman-20260331.raw.tsv` and `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-aliyun-podman-20260331.median.tsv`. This host is cleaner than the earlier VMware and Hyper-V VMs in CPU topology, but the current mounted disk path still imposes noticeable small-sync-write cost, so the results should be treated as host-specific native amd64 data rather than a final architecture verdict.
2026-03-31: Added `build-arm/perf-amd64-host-compare-20260331.md`, a consolidated comparison table for the three evaluated native amd64 hosts (`10.202.3.45`, `10.202.3.43`, `39.106.7.212`). The table summarizes host configuration, disk-path behavior from the lightweight `dd` checks, and available Tendis `SET p1` benchmark data, explicitly marking `10.202.3.43` as not benchmarked because it was rejected on storage-path grounds.
2026-03-31: Rechecked `10.202.3.43` after the user questioned the earlier storage diagnosis. The rerun reproduced the same pattern: small `4K dsync` writes remained extremely slow, while large sequential `fdatasync` writes stayed fast. This confirms the host's storage path is consistently unsuitable for write-heavy Tendis benchmarking rather than the earlier measurement being a one-off outlier.
2026-03-31: Despite the poor `4K dsync` diagnostic on `10.202.3.43`, the user asked to run a formal Tendis `SET p1` benchmark anyway. Uploaded the local amd64 server and memtier images, switched from rootless podman to `sudo podman` because the user session lacked the required cgroup `cpu` controller, and completed a 30s warmup plus 3 x 30s measured trials. The host delivered materially higher `SET p1` throughput than the earlier storage probe suggested, so the quick disk test alone was too pessimistic for this specific workload. Detailed result artifacts remain on the remote host under `/tmp/write-amd64-hyperv-setp1-20260331`.
2026-03-31: Saved the Hyper-V host `SET p1` result to `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-hyperv-setp1-20260331.md` with companion result tables `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-hyperv-setp1-20260331.raw.tsv` and `build-arm/perf-amd64-native-hyperv-setp1-20260331.median.tsv`, then updated `build-arm/perf-amd64-host-compare-20260331.md` so the three-host comparison now includes the measured `SET p1` throughput from `10.202.3.43` instead of the earlier `N/A` placeholder.
